NAME
       getfacl - show the access control lists (ACLs) of files

SYNOPSIS
       getfacl [-a] [-d] [-c] [-e] [-n] [-p] [-R] [-t] FILE...

DESCRIPTION
       Besides the owner, group and other permissions, a file or directory
       can have an ACL that gives extra users and groups their own
       permissions. getfacl prints the ACL of each FILE:

           # file: srv/project
           # owner: root
           # group: devops
           user::rwx
           user:student:rwx
           group::r-x
           mask::rwx
           other::---
           default:user::rwx
           default:user:student:rwx
           ...

   Reading the entries
       user::     the owner's permissions
       user:NAME: a named user's permissions
       group::    the owning group's permissions
       group:NAME:
                  a named group's permissions
       mask::     the most that named users, named groups and the owning
                  group can get. If a line shows #effective:r--, the mask
                  cuts that entry's permissions down to these.
       other::    everybody else
       default:   on a directory only: the ACL that new files and
                  subdirectories created in it get (inherited ACL)

       ls -l shows a + after the permission string when a file has an ACL,
       and then the group permissions it shows are the mask.

       Set ACLs with setfacl. Leading slashes are removed from the names in
       the output (so it can be fed back to setfacl --restore).

OPTIONS
       -a, --access
              Show only the access ACL.

       -d, --default
              Show only the default ACL.

       -c, --omit-header
              Leave out the # file, # owner and # group lines.

       -e, --all-effective
              Show the effective rights on every entry.

       -E, --no-effective
              Never show effective rights.

       -n, --numeric
              Print UIDs and GIDs instead of names.

       -p, --absolute-names
              Keep the leading slash.

       -R, --recursive
              List directories and everything in them.

       -t, --tabular
              A table with the access and default ACL side by side.

       -s, --skip-base
              Skip files that only have the base entries (no real ACL).

EXAMPLES
       Look at the ACL of a shared directory:

           getfacl /srv/project

       Check one user's entry in a script:

           getfacl /srv/project | grep '^user:student:'

       Save the ACLs of a tree and put them back later:

           getfacl -R /srv/project > /root/project.acl
           setfacl --restore=/root/project.acl

       The usual pairing: give a user rights and make new files inherit them:

           setfacl -m u:student:rwx /srv/project
           setfacl -m d:u:student:rwx /srv/project
           getfacl /srv/project

SEE ALSO
       setfacl(1), acl(5), chmod(1), ls(1)
